Attributions
============

Stem MCP (tolutronics/audio-processing-mcp), MIT License per its README
  https://github.com/tolutronics/audio-processing-mcp
  Derived ideas: the MCP server skeleton (a low-level mcp.server.Server with
  list_tools / call_tool handlers served over stdio) in
  src/trackcard/mcp_server.py, and the idea of running Demucs htdemucs to split
  a bounce into stems (src/trackcard/stems.py). No code was copied. trackcard
  does not use Stem MCP's analyze_audio, create_loop or separate_vocal_ranges.

MixMind (Intellligent15/MixMind--AI-DJ), no license file
  https://github.com/Intellligent15/MixMind--AI-DJ
  No code was copied. trackcard reimplements, in its own code, methods that
  MixMind's analysis and stem services describe: tempo octave correction with
  x0.5 / x2/3 / x1.5 / x2 hints outside 85-170 BPM; downbeat phase from a
  sub-250 Hz onset envelope; key detection with HPSS, chroma_cens, RMS
  weighting, Krumhansl-Schmuckler and a relative major/minor tiebreak;
  allin1-first section detection with a Laplacian segmentation fallback; and
  Demucs htdemucs with a GPU-to-CPU fallback chain and 10 Hz RMS/peak
  envelopes.

Published methods and models used
=================================
- Krumhansl & Kessler (1982) key profiles.
- Temperley (1999), "What's key for key? The Krumhansl-Schmuckler key-finding
  algorithm reconsidered" (second-opinion key profiles).
- McFee & Ellis (2014), "Analyzing song structure with spectral clustering"
  (Laplacian segmentation), via librosa.
- Demucs / Hybrid Transformer Demucs (Defossez et al., Meta AI), MIT License.
  Model weights are downloaded by the demucs package on first use.
- All-In-One music structure analyzer, allin1 (Taejun Kim), optional.
- pyloudnorm (ITU-R BS.1770 loudness), optional.
